WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.671
In a dense layer, every token runs through
every parameter.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.640
A mixture of experts keeps eight copies of
that layer instead of one.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.915
A small router reads the token, and scores
all eight.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.233
The best two win.

5
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:16.988
The other six are never touched.

6
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.188
Only those two do any arithmetic.

7
00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:22.247
That is where the word sparse lives.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.047
All eight still sit in memory.

9
00:00:25.047 --> 00:00:28.249
Parameter count and compute stop being the
same number.

10
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.564
A second token arrives, and the router
picks a different pair.

11
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.508
Across a batch, the work spreads over all
of them.

12
00:00:36.508 --> 00:00:37.987
Per token, it never does.

13
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.076
But routing feeds back on itself.

14
00:00:41.076 --> 00:00:44.575
An expert that wins early wins more, and
the rest starve.

15
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:51.758
So noise is added to the scores to keep it
exploring, and the loss gets a term that penalises the pile-up.

16
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:53.960
Capacity you store.

17
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:54.997
Compute you skip.
